  "worked_solution": "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=2,$ we plug $a=2$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(2)+{f}^{\u2032}(2)(x-2)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{\u2033}(2){(x-2)}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(2),$ ${f}^{\u2032}(2),$ and ${f}^{\u2033}(2):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4}{x} & \u27f9 &  & f(2) & =2 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}} & \u27f9 &  & {f}^{\u2032}(2) & =-1 \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}} & \u27f9 &  & {f}^{\u2033}(2) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(2),$ ${f}^{\u2032}(2),$ and ${f}^{\u2033}(2)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =2+(-1)(x-2)+1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{(x-2)}^{2} \\\\  & =2-1(x-2)+\\frac{1}{2}{(x-2)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
